<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">National Convention</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1792–1795</span> · Also known as French National Convention</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National Convention was the elected assembly that governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from September 1792 to October 1795. On its first full day it abolished the monarchy and proclaimed the French Republic, and in 1793 it tried and executed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a>. Under pressure of war and revolt it gave power to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/committee-of-public-safety">Committee of Public Safety</a>, which ran the Terror, and after Robespierre's fall it drafted the constitution of the Directory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-convention.jpg" alt="National Convention" width="320" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fluffy89502 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:French_National_Convention_Composition_Pre-purge.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
